ating for  a new convention  how would a constitutional convention  work  we haven't had a constitutional  convention since 1787 so it's hard to  say  however given the country's intense  political disagreements it seems safe to  assume that the process could be chaotic  and divisive especially since there are  no clear ground rules  for example there are no rules limiting  a constitutional convention to a single  topic so even if States called a  convention to propose say a balanced  budget amendment it's entirely possible  that the convention could propose  something else instead  it's also not clear whether Congress or  the states or even someone else would  get to select the delegates to a  convention  and if or how they could try to control  them  some commentators in fact point out that  the Constitutional Convention of 1787  was originally called only to modify the  Articles of Confederation  instead of tweaking the Articles the  framers did away with them entirely and  replaced them with something completely  different  there is therefore a precedent for rogue  delegates to a constitutional convention  going beyond their original mandate and  drafting a charter for a totally new  government  however a Constitutional Convention May  play out it is clear that it would be an  intensely political process  and while it might improve the existing  Constitution could also make things  worse to be sure a constitutional  convention would invite much needed  discussion about the strengths and  weaknesses of our current constitutional  order  but we should also be very cautious  before throwing it away  for all its problems the United States  is comparatively very prosperous and  stable  our constitution is also the world's  oldest written Constitution still in use  today  that longevity serves us well because it  helps ensure that people respect the  document  Americans accept the constraints the  Constitution imposes on their leaders  and the rights the Constitution 