 was that the ideological balance of the  nation's most important Court was at  stake  faced with a senate that was promising  obstructionism President Obama decided  to nominate about the least  controversial candidate imaginable  he selected chief judge Merrick garland  from the U.S court of appeals for the  D.C circuit  even in a crowded field of exemplary  candidates chief judge Garland stood out  highly respected by lawyers all across  the political Spectrum judge Garland had  an impeccable reputation as a brilliant  meticulous hard-working fair-minded  nonpartisan judge  a Harvard Law graduate and a former  Supreme Court Clerk chief judge Garland  also had a sterling resume including  stints in private practice teaching at  Harvard Law School and working at the  Department of Justice where he headed  the team that prosecuted the  perpetrators of the Oklahoma City  bombings in the mid-1990s  he was also the chief judge of the DC  circuit which is sometimes referred to  as the nation's second most important  court because it hears many of the cases  involving federal administrative  agencies  perhaps even more importantly chief  judge Garland was considered such a  fair-minded judge that some Senate  Republicans had indicated a few years  earlier that if President Obama were to  nominate him for a different Supreme  Court opening they would support him  President Obama no doubt selected judge  Garland precisely because he wanted a  consensus candidate that both  Republicans and Democrats could support  in the interest of full disclosure I  should acknowledge here that I had the  Good Fortune of clerking for judge  Garland for a year  like everyone else I was extraordinarily  impressed with his Fierce intelligence  his tireless work ethic and above all  else his sense of fairness I never just  saw him decide a case based on  pre-formed ideology  true to the early promise however Senate  Republicans refused to give chief judge  Garland either a hearing or a vote  these tactics dismayed me as in my view  the Senate was refusing even to consider  a person who could not possibly be  better qualified to sit on the Supreme  Court  but from a political standpoint the  Republicans Gambit made sense  the late Justice Scalia had been a very  reliable conservative vote  even if judge Garland were a perfectly  Centrist judge his appointment would  swing the court away from the right as  it would replace a very conservative  Justice with a moderate one  Democrats cried foul the Republicans  Behavior broke with the long-standing  practice of giving some deference to the  president's Supreme Court nominee  even when the Democrats rejected  President Reagan's nominee judge Robert  borked to the Supreme Court in the 1980s  they still provided a hearing and a vote  in fact their decision to vote no one  judge bork was based substantially on  what he had said during his confirmation  hearings  and the Democrats view fairly or not  judge bork had espoused an aggressively  conservative rigidly ideological  approach to the law  by contrast judge Garland was offered no  such opportunity to share his Judicial  philosophy with the country  some Democrats contended that Senate  Republicans were acting  unconstitutionally  the argument in effect was that the  constitution grants the president the  authority to appoint federal judges and  the Senate was denying the president his  constitutional prerogative  Democrats further contended that  Republicans argument that they should  wait until after the election to choose  the next Justice had no logical stopping  point  Justice Scalia died nearly nine months  before the election should the Senate  refuse to consider judicial nominees for  vacancies occurring a year before a  presidential election two years  Democrats constitutional protestations  mostly fell on death years  Constitution entrust the Senate with  providing advice and consent for the  president's Federal judicial nominees  though the Senate is often more  respectful of the president's selection  than it was to judge Garland the  Constitution effectively delegates the  confirmation process to the Senate  where the law leaves a matter in the  discretion of a political body one  cannot use the courts to try to change  that body's process and decision  if the Democrats were going to get judge  Garland through  they would have to do it using politics  not law  there seemed to be some reason to think  that the Democrats would succeed in  their efforts  at the time the Republican strategy  seemed risky  Hillary Clinton was the Democratic  Party's candidate for president and she  seemed likely to defeat the Republicans  upstart candidate Real Estate Mogul and  reality TV star Donald Trump  for most of their race she was  substantially ahead in the polls  were Clinton to win the presidency there  was no guarantee she would stick with  judge Garland who was then 63 years old  President Clinton might decide to  withdraw judge Garland's nomination and  choose instead in ideological liberal  who would try to steer the court  aggressively to the left and a much  younger person who might stay in the  court longer  furthermore Republicans refusal to Grant  even a hearing to judge Garland seem to  feed into the Democrats campaign message  that the Republicans were obstructionist  and appeared as though it could alienate  key swing voters in the upcoming  election  but the Republicans gamble of course  paid off Donald Trump won the presidency  and with it the chance to select Justice  scalia's replacement  moreover Republicans held on to their  control of the Senate so president Trump  had his pick of conservatives  he ended up selecting judge Neil Gorsuch  from the U.S court of appeals for the  10th circuit  like chief judge Garland judge Gorsuch  enjoyed a sterling reputation as a  highly regarded judge with an impressive  pedigree and legal mind  but there can be no dispute that  President Trump also chose him because  of his reputation as an ideological  conservative  indeed the folks advising president  Trump on this election including the  influential Federalist Society wanted  just that someone who would consistently  Advance their interests and ideologies  at the Supreme Court  and the Senate of course confirmed judge  Gorsuch as the new Justice  as of this taping the country is now  engaged in another confirmation battle  Justice Anthony Kennedy for years the  Supreme Court Swing Vote has announced  his retirement giving president Trump  his second Supreme Court vacancy to fill  in less than two years  president Trump promised to replace  Justice Kennedy with a committed  conservative in the mold of Justice  Gorsuch  the replacement of the Court's swing  Justice with a consistent conservative  would of course shift the court  substantially to the right  unsurprisingly Democrats have vowed to  fight an ideological conservative  nominee but as Republicans control the  Senate Democrats would need to pick off  some Republican votes to defeat the  nominee  notice that politics rather than  principal guides Senators approaches to  these matters  Democrats who had cried foul when the  Republicans insisted on postponing any  hearing on Judge Garland until after the  election argued in summer 2018 that the  Senate should not vote on a new Justice  until after the November midterm  elections  and Republicans who had said that there  shouldn't be a hearing or a vote on  Judge Garland until after the election  now wants to complete the process as  quickly as possible lest they risk  losing their majority in the election  with these confirmation Battle episodes  show rather starkly I think is that  constitutional law and politics are  inextricably light  the justices themselves May usually try  to apply the law neutrally and  impartially  but their own background views matter  sometimes a great deal  understanding this very well presidents  and Senators support the appointment of  justices whom they believe will vote in  ways that tend to support their own  political viewpoints  it's understandable that the public  sometimes gets the misimpression that  what goes on at the court is politics  and nothing else  political disagreements in American  society have been Fierce since the  nation's founding but they have produced  such vitriol and distrust in recent  years that our political system strikes  many people as dysfunctional  Democrats and Republicans often treat  each other as enemies  this atmosphere makes political  compromise extremely challenging  as the nation looks for Solutions a  logical question is whether the  Constitution itself which lays the  ground rules for our political system  deserves some of the blame for these  problems  it has long been heretical to criticize  the Constitution it is in some ways  America's Civic religion  but a blind faith is not a deep one and  any serious study of the Constitution  should consider which parts are working  well and which ones are not  naturally liberals and conservatives  tend to lament different aspects of our  constitutional law  some conservatives want to amend the  Constitution to provide further  protection for religion or to remove Roe  v Wade's protection of abortion  perhaps conservatives most fundamental  laments though involve the scope of the  federal government and the argument that  it has grown far beyond the framer's  original intentions and the fact that  federal spending and debt have gotten  out of control  for their part many liberals might favor  their own constitutional revisions  many of these pertain to voting and the  political process such as outlawing  partisan gerrymandering protecting the  right to vote and limiting corporations  ability to influence elections through  campaign contributions and election year  and Communications  other liberal objections implicate the  structure of government more generally  the makeup of the Senate some point out  is profoundly undemocratic giving each  state two senators regardless of  population size  the result is that California and  Wyoming enjoy equal power in the Senate  even though the population of California  is close to 70 times that of Wyoming  of course Article 5 of The Constitution  stipulates that no state without its  consent shall be deprived of equal  representation in the Senate so it would  be impossible to alter the makeup of the  Senate without scrappling the  constitution in its entirety  but liberals nevertheless point to the  Senate as evidence that the constitution  is far less Democratic than Americans  sometimes like to think it is  liberals and some conservatives have  similar complaints about the  constitutionally mandated electoral  college process for electing the  president  under Article 2 of The Constitution the  president is elected not by national  popular vote but through elections in  each state  each state is given a certain number of  electoral College votes equal to the  size of the Congressional Delegation  from that state  in other words each state gets the same  number of electoral College votes as the  number of its House and Senate members  combined  as critics have observed this system  gives disproportionate weight to states  with low population since they have  Senate membership disproportionate to  their size  whether or not one likes this  Arrangement it's also undemocratic as it  gives people in some states greater vote  and power than people and others  this electoral college system also has  other consequences  for example the vast majority of states  have a winner take all system so that a  candidate who only narrowly wins a  majority in a state still gets all of  that State's Electoral College votes  as a result candidates tend to focus  their campaigning on key States usually  so-called swing states that could  possibly go either way in an election  candidates spend much less time and  fewer resources on states that they are  assured to win or lose  and they have a strong incentive to make  campaign promises designed to sway  voters in those swing States promises  that can have a significant impact on  the policies of the new Administration  another effect of the electoral college  system is that it can result in  candidates winning the presidency  despite having lost the popular election  for example George W Bush in 2000 and  Donald Trump in 2016 both lost the  popular vote but won the presidency  because they won more Electoral College  votes  the partisan Rancor over these outcomes  especially the 2000 election which  culminated in litigation about a recount  in Florida was intense and Lasting  some critics contend that the losing  side would have an easier time accepting  the results of an election that turned  only on the popular vote  attributing to the nation's political  dysfunction should it be changed  as you may know Article 5 of The  Constitution provides two ways of  proposing a constitutional amendment  the first requires a two-thirds vote by  each house of Congress  the second requires two-thirds of state  legislatures to request that Congress  call for a convention which would then  debate and propose Constitutional  Amendments  in either case a proposed Constitutional  Amendment would need to be ratified by  three-quarters of the states  all the amendments to the U.S  Constitution that have been made to date  have gone through the first process  but a movement is now underway to call a  constitutional convention using the  second process with the goals of  limiting the scope of federal power and  requiring a balanced federal budget  while limiting Federal power and  requiring a balanced budget or  conservative agenda items the idea of a  new Constitutional Convention also has  notable liberal supporters  in particular Sanford Levinson who  teaches at the University of Texas law  school and is one of the great  constitutional Scholars of his  generation has long been advocating for  a new convention  how would a constitutional convention  work  we haven't had a constitutional  convention since 1787 so it's hard to  say  however given the country's intense  political disagreements it seems safe to  assume that the process could be chaotic  and divisive especially since there are  no clear ground rules  for example there are no rules limiting  a constitutional convention to a single  topic so even if States called a  convention to propose say a balanced  budget amendment it's entirely possible  that the convention could propose  something else instead  it's also not clear whether Congress or  the states or even someone else would  get to select the delegates to a  convention  and if or how they could try to control  them  some commentators in fact point out that  the Constitutional Convention of 1787  was originally called only to modify the  Articles of Confederation  instead of tweaking the Articles the  framers did away with them entirely and  replaced them with something completely  different  there is therefore a precedent for rogue  delegates to a constitutional convention  going beyond their original mandate and  drafting a charter for a totally new  government  however a Constitutional Convention May  play out it is clear that it would be an  intensely political process  and while it might improve the existing  Constitution could also make things  worse to be sure a constitutional  convention would invite much needed  discussion about the strengths and  weaknesses of our current constitutional  order  but we should also be very cautious  before throwing it away  for all its problems the United States  is comparatively very prosperous and  stable  our constitution is also the world's  oldest written Constitution still in use  today  that longevity serves us well because it  helps ensure that people respect the  document  Americans accept the constraints the  Constitution imposes on their leaders  and the rights the Constitution grants  were the document to change dramatically  people might view the new version  differently and commitment to the rule  of law might suffer  in particular a convention's losers  might deem the end result illegitimate  if they thought that folks on the other  side had gained the process in their own  favor  so is constitutional law just politics  by another name  I wouldn't go that far but I do think  that constitutional law and politics are  inseparable  this is not to say that there is no such  thing as constitutional law or that  constitutional interpretation is just  thinly veiled political policy making  there is constitutional law  it exists in the text of the document  such as the requirement that the  president be at least 35 years old  it exists in the decisions of the U.S  Supreme Court which comprise precedent  that is binding on Lower federal courts  and state courts  and it exists in the practices of  governmental officials who take an oath  to abide by the Constitution and usually  believe themselves to be the  Constitution's faithful stewards  these elements however cannot be  understood in a political vacuum  the Constitution helps Define how  American politics operate  and these politics in turn shape the  content of our constitutional law such  as through the judicial confirmation  process  you cannot understand constitutional Law  Without some political insights but you  also can't understand U.S politics  without understanding the Constitution  it's partially for this reason that as  the nation has changed over time so too  is the Constitution  through formal Article 5 Amendment  through change judicial interpretations  and through change governmental and  societal practices  the Constitution is far from perfect and  as the framers recognized it never was  there's much to criticize about the  constitution of 1787. most obviously its  despicable pro-slavery provisions  but there is also something to admire  about many framers willingness to  compromise their ideals so that they  could create a new governmental order  that was a big Improvement on what had  come before it  at various points in our history the  country has lost this ability to  compromise including in its debates  about the meaning of the Constitution  as we continue to debate how to meet the  challenges of today and tomorrow  I hope that we like the founders will  try to listen to each other's views  about how to build a more perfect union
 

